linked-list-cycle-merge.promptSeed:
Implement cycle detection and sorted list merging on singly linked list nodes (`{ val, next }`).

#### How a Singly Linked List Works
A singly linked list is a chain of `{ val, next }` node objects accessed via a pointer to the first node (`head`, or `null` if the list is empty). `head.val` holds the first node's value, `head.next` points to the second node, and each `.next` links onward until the last node's `.next` is `null`:

```linked-list
{
  "rows": [
    {
      "caption": "list(1, 2, 3, 4) — head points to node 1; the last node points to null:",
      "nodes": [
        { "val": 1, "pointers": ["head"], "state": "active" },
        { "val": 2 },
        { "val": 3 },
        { "val": 4 }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

```linked-list
{
  "title": "Cycle Example (h.next.next.next.next = h.next)",
  "rows": [
    {
      "caption": "Node 4's .next points back to node 2 (h.next) instead of null, creating a cycle:",
      "nodes": [
        { "val": 1, "pointers": ["h"] },
        { "val": 2, "pointers": ["h.next"], "state": "cycle" },
        { "val": 3 },
        { "val": 4, "state": "cycle" }
      ],
      "cycleToIndex": 1
    }
  ]
}
```

### `hasCycle<T>(head: ListNode<T> | null): boolean`
- Detects whether a linked list contains a cycle using Floyd's Tortoise and Hare algorithm in $O(1)$ auxiliary space.
- Returns `true` if any node can be reached more than once by following `next` pointers; otherwise `false`.

### `mergeTwoLists(a: ListNode<number> | null, b: ListNode<number> | null): ListNode<number> | null`
- Merges two sorted linked lists into a single sorted list by relinking existing nodes (without allocating new node objects).
- Either list `a` or `b` may be `null` or empty.
- When values tie (`a.val === b.val`), takes from list `a` first.
- Returns the head of the merged sorted list.

